  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "prose",
      "caption": "Summary",
      "html": "<p>Most nouns of the first declension are feminine and end in -α or -η. Their endings are close to those of the second declension, with α or η where the second declension has ο.</p>"
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "caption": "ὥρα, ἀλήθεια, δόξα, γραφή",
      "columns": [
        "Case",
        "ὥρα",
        "ἀλήθεια",
        "δόξα",
        "γραφή"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "Nom. sg.",
          "ὥρα",
          "ἀλήθεια",
          "δόξα",
          "γραφή"
        ],
        [
          "Gen. sg.",
          "ὥρας",
          "ἀληθείας",
          "δόξης",
          "γραφῆς"
        ],
        [
          "Dat. sg.",
          "ὥρᾳ",
          "ἀληθείᾳ",
          "δόξῃ",
          "γραφῇ"
        ],
        [
          "Acc. sg.",
          "ὥραν",
          "ἀλήθειαν",
          "δόξαν",
          "γραφήν"
        ],
        [
          "Nom. pl.",
          "ὧραι",
          "ἀλήθειαι",
          "δόξαι",
          "γραφαί"
        ],
        [
          "Gen. pl.",
          "ὡρῶν",
          "ἀληθειῶν",
          "δοξῶν",
          "γραφῶν"
        ],
        [
          "Dat. pl.",
          "ὥραις",
          "ἀληθείαις",
          "δόξαις",
          "γραφαῖς"
        ],
        [
          "Acc. pl.",
          "ὥρας",
          "ἀληθείας",
          "δόξας",
          "γραφάς"
        ],
        [
          "After ε, ι or ρ the α stays through the singular (ὥρα, ἡμέρα); otherwise a short α of the nominative becomes η in the genitive and dative singular (δόξα, δόξης). The plural is the same for all of them."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "prose",
      "caption": "Genitive plural",
      "html": "<p>Every first-declension noun has a circumflex on the ultima in the genitive plural, wherever the nominative is accented: ὡρῶν, ἡμερῶν, γραφῶν.</p>"
    },
    {
      "type": "prose",
      "caption": "Common confusion",
      "html": "<p>ὥρας can be genitive singular or accusative plural; ψυχαί is plural, ψυχῇ dative singular. Let the sentence decide.</p>"
    },
    {
      "type": "prose",
      "caption": "Source",
      "html": "<p>Machen, *New Testament Greek for Beginners* (1923), Lesson 5 (p. 29).</p>"
    },
